 {
  "id": "R-PHARMA-RPHARMA-2025-RINPHARMA_552",
  "title": "MedxR Package - Bridging Regulatory Drug Data from the FDA and Health Canada into R",
  "authors": [
   "Renzo Cáceres Rossi"
  ],
  "year": 2025,
  "conference": "r-pharma",
  "conference_name": "R/Pharma",
  "section_code": "TK",
  "section_name": "Talk",
  "paper_code": "RPharma-2025-rinpharma_552",
  "pdf_url": "https://youtu.be/3_dNFyvv9hY",
  "source_url": "https://rinpharma.github.io/data-pipelines/output/processed_talks.csv",
  "abstract": "In this talk, I will introduce MedxR, a new R package that connects directly to public regulatory APIs from the U.S. FDA and Health Canada. MedxR allows users to programmatically search, retrieve, and analyze drug approval data, recall alerts, product labels, and ingredient information. This tool is designed to support pharmacovigilance, drug research, academic use, and real-world data analysis in regulatory science. I will demonstrate key functions such as querying drug products, tracking recalls, and comparing medication records across both agencies",
  "keywords": [],
  "lang": "en",
  "region": "Global",
  "added_at": "2026-08-06T08:04:34+00:00"
 },
 {
  "id": "R-PHARMA-RPHARMA-2025-RINPHARMA_553",
  "title": "risk.assessr:  extending its use in the package validation process",
  "authors": [
   "Hugo Bottois"
  ],
  "year": 2025,
  "conference": "r-pharma",
  "conference_name": "R/Pharma",
  "section_code": "TK",
  "section_name": "Talk",
  "paper_code": "RPharma-2025-rinpharma_553",
  "pdf_url": "https://youtu.be/LHMZNZY1Pt8",
  "source_url": "https://rinpharma.github.io/data-pipelines/output/processed_talks.csv",
  "abstract": "The Sanofi R Validation team is currently creating a number of cutting edge GitHub processes to employ the risk.assessr package to help Sanofi effectively manage risks associated with internal, third-party and open-source packages.    The first process is a database to store the risk metric data (RCMD check, test coverage, documentation metrics, traceability matrix), create Sanofi risk thresholds, and generate documentation for package acceptance/rejection for the team to make a decision about a target package. The process also populates the documentation with any metrics that do not pass the Sanofi criteria.   Next is a GitHub CICD process to run risk.assessr on an individual target package for a specific package evaluation and store that data in the DB. The final process is a GitHub CICD process to run the risk.assessr functionality to process renv.lock files that contain a target package and its dependencies and store the data collected in the DB.",
  "keywords": [],
  "lang": "en",
  "region": "Global",
  "added_at": "2026-08-06T08:04:34+00:00"
 },
 {
  "id": "R-PHARMA-RPHARMA-2025-RINPHARMA_554",
  "title": "Code Review for Compliance: Best Practices for Validated R Workflows in Pharma",
  "authors": [
   "Alexandros Kouretsis"
  ],
  "year": 2025,
  "conference": "r-pharma",
  "conference_name": "R/Pharma",
  "section_code": "TK",
  "section_name": "Talk",
  "paper_code": "RPharma-2025-rinpharma_554",
  "pdf_url": "https://youtu.be/8xsqfilCIzk",
  "source_url": "https://rinpharma.github.io/data-pipelines/output/processed_talks.csv",
  "abstract": "Code review is a mandatory step in the development of validated R packages used in regulated pharmaceutical environments. As R continues to gain ground across clinical and regulatory domains, structured review processes are critical to ensure clarity, reproducibility, compliance, and long-term maintainability. This talk presents a practical framework for integrating code review into R workflows, informed by my experience as a contributor and reviewer with rOpenSci and regulated industry projects. Attendees will gain actionable strategies to foster a strong review culture, reduce risk, and build trustworthy, validation-ready software that meets the rigorous standards of pharma.",
  "keywords": [],
  "lang": "en",
  "region": "Global",
  "added_at": "2026-08-06T08:04:34+00:00"
 },
 {
  "id": "R-PHARMA-RPHARMA-2025-RINPHARMA_555",
  "title": "A Web-Based R Application for Forecasting Patient Enrollment in Clinical Trials",
  "authors": [
   "Akifumi Okayama",
   "Motoki Oe",
   "Nobushige Matsuoka"
  ],
  "year": 2025,
  "conference": "r-pharma",
  "conference_name": "R/Pharma",
  "section_code": "TK",
  "section_name": "Talk",
  "paper_code": "RPharma-2025-rinpharma_555",
  "pdf_url": "",
  "source_url": "https://rinpharma.github.io/data-pipelines/output/processed_talks.csv",
  "abstract": "Whether a clinical trial is completed on schedule is a critical concern—it not only affects the management of study timelines and costs, but also significantly impacts the timing of regulatory submissions, market strategy, and post-approval revenue. Among the various phases, patient enrollment often accounts for the majority of the trial duration and is particularly difficult to predict due to its inherent uncertainty. As a result, predicting patient enrollment is both highly important and particularly challenging.  To address this challenge, statistical models have been proposed to describe and forecast enrollment dynamics. Among them, the Poisson-Gamma model, introduced by Anisimov & Fedorov (2007), is one of the most prominent. This model treats site-specific enrollment rates as gamma-distributed random variables and combines them with a Poisson process, enabling flexible modeling that accounts for heterogeneity in enrollment rates across sites. In recent years, more complex extensions of this model have also become feasible for practical use.  In practice, enrollment operations are managed by clinical operations teams, and models that fail to incorporate their insights may end up as theoretical exercises with limited practical value. At the same time, expecting operations staff to learn R or statistical modeling is unrealistic, and having statisticians accompany them for every use case is inefficient.  This presentation introduces a case study of a web-based application that enables intuitive use of enrollment prediction models without requiring R expertise. The application adopts a decoupled architecture, separating the server-side and front-end components. On the server side, the statistical model is implemented in R and exposed as an API using the plumber package, then hosted on Posit Connect. The front end, built with HTML, CSS, and JavaScript, accesses the API to retrieve model outputs and visualizations, which are then displayed in the browser. A web application removes the need for local installation and encourages real-world adoption.  The talk will primarily highlight technical challenges, architectural decisions, and implementation strategies encountered by statisticians in the pharmaceutical industry. A live demonstration of the current alpha version is also planned.",
  "keywords": [],
  "lang": "en",
  "region": "Global",
  "added_at": "2026-08-06T08:04:34+00:00"
 },
 {
  "id": "R-PHARMA-RPHARMA-2025-RINPHARMA_556",
